    straightforward syntactic embedding can interact unexpectedly with other
    language constructs.}
}

@Book{Kaisler2024,
  author={Stephen H. Kaisler},
  title={Medley LOOPS: The Basic System},
  year={2024},
  month=jul,
  volume={1},
  series={Medley LOOPS},
  edition={Version 1.2},
  publisher={The Medley Interlisp Project},
  url={https://interlisp.org/documentation/2024-loops-book-1.pdf},
  note={
    Modern documentation of LOOPS, the Lisp Object-Oriented Programming System
    originally developed at Xerox PARC as an extension of Interlisp.
    Describes its classes, instances, metaclasses, message sending, methods,
    multiple inheritance and superclass precedence, as well as its unusual
    active-value and rule-oriented facilities.
    Particularly useful as a detailed operational reference for the revived
    system, though written retrospectively rather than as a contemporary
    account of the original LOOPS design.
  }
}

@WebPage{Krishnamurthi2024,
  author={Shriram Krishnamurthi},
  title={Bicameral, Not Homoiconic},
  year={2024},
  organization={Parenthetically Speaking},
  url={https://parentheticallyspeaking.org/articles/bicameral-not-homoiconic/},
  lastchecked={2026-09-06},
  note={Argues that ``homoiconicity'' is too vague to explain what is distinctive
    about Lisp syntax, and proposes ``bicameral syntax'' instead: a reader first
    constructs a well-formed tree, after which a parser checks language-specific
    validity.
    Particularly relevant for distinguishing reader-level tree structure from
    abstract syntax, and for explaining why Lisp-family macro systems benefit
    from a structured intermediate representation without requiring literal
    code-as-data identity.}
}

@WebPage{Ma2024,
  author={Noah Ma},
  title={Category Theory in Programming},
  year={2024},
  organization={Racket},
  url={https://docs.racket-lang.org/ctp/index.html},
  lastchecked={2026-09-06},
  note={
    Tutorial connecting category-theoretic concepts with concrete programming
    constructs in Racket.
    Develops executable representations of categories, functors, natural
    transformations and related constructions, emphasizing their interpretation
    as programming abstractions rather than category theory in isolation.
    Useful as a programming-oriented exposition with runnable examples.
  }
}

